\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Leave-one-model-out evaluation. ``LOO-across-models'' trains on 6 targets, tests on the 7th. Mean across 7 folds: \textbf  {64.2\%} [59--69\%], within 3.0\,pp of the pooled within-sample LOO (67.1\%). The fold-level spread is wide (52.0--76.3\%), so this indicates \textbf  {weak cross-model transfer} rather than reliable generalization: the mean is close to the pooled figure while individual held-out targets range from chance to substantially above it. T-acc = truthful-trial accuracy; L-acc = lying-trial accuracy. The Llama~4~Maverick pipeline result (45\%) is 19\,pp below the worst in-distribution fold, consistent with out-of-distribution failure, though with one frontier target we cannot separate distribution shift from model-specific idiosyncrasy.}}{29}{table.caption.68}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:loo_across_models}{{18}{29}{Leave-one-model-out evaluation. ``LOO-across-models'' trains on 6 targets, tests on the 7th. Mean across 7 folds: \textbf {64.2\%} [59--69\%], within 3.0\,pp of the pooled within-sample LOO (67.1\%). The fold-level spread is wide (52.0--76.3\%), so this indicates \textbf {weak cross-model transfer} rather than reliable generalization: the mean is close to the pooled figure while individual held-out targets range from chance to substantially above it. T-acc = truthful-trial accuracy; L-acc = lying-trial accuracy. The Llama~4~Maverick pipeline result (45\%) is 19\,pp below the worst in-distribution fold, consistent with out-of-distribution failure, though with one frontier target we cannot separate distribution shift from model-specific idiosyncrasy}{table.caption.68}{}}
